![](/images/articles/off-lawn.jpeg)

Grandpa Steve has poured himself two fingers of bourbon, kicked back in his rocking chair, and is prepared to drool some knowledge.

This rant is inspired by an email I got yesterday. I didn't read the email.

![](/images/articles/get-off-my-lawn-five-unpardonable-prospecting-sins/off%20lawn%20in%20article.png)

My email preview displays fifteen words. The next time you send a prospecting email, ask yourself if the first fifteen words have earned you the continued attention of your reader. Here are five unpardonable sins I routinely see in prospecting emails.

- Don't tell me that you did research on my company. Tell me something I don't know that resulted from your research.
- Don't tell me that you looked at my background. That information is useless to me (and is a little creepy). Show me that you understand what I care about.
- Don't tell me that you would like to meet or connect. I don't know you, and I don't give a flying [fork](https://thegoodplace.fandom.com/wiki/Censored_Curse_Words) what you would like. Prove that you have something valuable to say.
- Don't tell me that you know I'm busy and that you don't want to waste my time. Show actual respect for my time by saying something useful.
- Don't make [grammatical errors](https://www.grammarly.com/blog/reflexive-pronouns/), especially in your first sentence.

And you meddling kids can get off my lawn.
